From: HP-UX 11.23 ia64 port notes
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] ia64-hpux: do not use @gprel64 for local symbols

TWO independent reasons, and the second is the important one because it applies
to EVERY linker, not just HP's. Both measured on rx2620.

1. HP ld B.12.34 mis-applies R_IA64_GPREL64I: it overwrites slot 0 of the MLX
   bundle as well as the L+X slots, destroying the "mov rNN = r1" GP save that
   lives there. Every function referencing a local symbol (any string literal)
   then dies with SIGILL. The object file is correct -- GNU as emits the right
   code and relocation -- the corruption appears only after HP ld runs.

2. ★ @gprel64 to anything OUTSIDE THE DATA SEGMENT IS INVALID IN A SHARED
   OBJECT ON THIS PLATFORM, for any linker. A gp-relative constant encodes the
   link-time distance S - GP, which survives loading only if the target and gp
   move together. On ia64-hpux they do not -- the text and data segments are
   mapped independently:

       .rodata   link 4000000000000250   run c000000005054250
       .data     link 6000000000000000   run 9fffffffef7f2008

   The distance is not preserved, so the immediate is wrong the moment the
   library loads, whatever value a linker wrote. No dynamic relocation can
   rescue it either: the loader relocates data words, not the 64-bit immediate
   inside an MLX bundle. Measured symptom: a function returning a pointer to
   its own string constant returns the LINK-TIME address and the caller
   SIGSEGVs. This is why `.sdata` access through gp works while `.rodata`
   access fails -- .sdata shares the segment with gp and .rodata does not.

gcc 4.7.4 emits @ltoff (LTOFF22X/LDXMOV) here and is correct on both counts;
gcc 9.5's @gprel64 choice is what makes its shared-library output unusable on
ia64-hpux. Falling through to the @ltoff path restores the working behaviour.
Cost: one extra load per local-symbol reference.

⇒ KEEP THIS PATCH PERMANENTLY FOR --enable-shared. It was retired 2026-07-31
on the evidence that the EXECUTABLE build completes without it, which remains
true -- executables load at fixed addresses so the distance is constant, and
cc1plus is the proof. Shared libraries are the case that needs it, and reason 2
means no future linker release will remove that need.

Scale of the problem without it: our gcc 9.5 libstdc++.a carries 1,857
GPREL64I relocations across 72 of its 183 objects.
--- a/gcc/config/ia64/ia64.c
+++ b/gcc/config/ia64/ia64.c
@@ -1137,13 +1137,23 @@
     emit_insn (gen_load_fptr (dest, src));
   else if (sdata_symbolic_operand (src, VOIDmode))
     emit_insn (gen_load_gprel (dest, src));
-  else if (local_symbolic_operand64 (src, VOIDmode))
+  else if (local_symbolic_operand64 (src, VOIDmode) && !TARGET_HPUX_LD)
     {
       /* We want to use @gprel rather than @ltoff relocations for local
 	 symbols:
 	  - @gprel does not require dynamic linker
 	  - and does not use .sdata section
-	 https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/60465 */
+	 https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/60465
+
+	 ia64-hpux: NOT on this target.  Two reasons, either sufficient.  (a) HP ld B.12.34
+	 mis-applies R_IA64_GPREL64I, overwriting slot 0 of the MLX bundle - which holds the
+	 "mov rNN = r1" GP save - so any local-symbol reference dies with SIGILL.  (b) More
+	 fundamentally, a gp-relative constant encodes the LINK-TIME distance S - GP, and on
+	 ia64-hpux the text and data segments are mapped INDEPENDENTLY at load time, so that
+	 distance is not preserved in a shared object.  No linker can write a correct value and
+	 no dynamic relocation can fix it, because the loader relocates data words rather than
+	 the 64-bit immediate inside an MLX bundle.  @ltoff (what gcc 4.7.4 emits) goes through
+	 the linkage table, which IS relocated, and is correct in both cases.
+	 Cost: one extra load per local-symbol reference.  */
       emit_insn (gen_load_gprel64 (dest, src));
     }
   else
